<freezeText>
## This action is only available to !ALWAYS_FEMALE PCs with FEMININITY < 50
You don't say anything.

This is not a decision. Your mouth is simply not open and no sound is coming out of it and that is what is happening. He has looked at you and said that thing and some part of your processing has gone briefly offline, and the part that's still running is watching the gap in the conversation stretch out and becoming dimly aware that the gap is now too long to fill without explanation.

He waits. He has the patient, slightly concerned expression of someone who's wondering if they've said something wrong.

You watch him reach a conclusion without you.

"Sorry," he says. "You really do look like her. I mean — him." He seems flustered now. "Sorry — I got confused, I don't know why I said — sorry to bother you."

He moves away toward the counter.

You look back down at your coffee. The surface of it is flat and still and in no way remarkable, and you concentrate on it for a second.

He thought he recognised you. He was right to think so and wrong in every specific and he's already decided he was wrong and that's — that's the thing. That's what just happened. He decided he was wrong on your behalf, without you having to do anything, because you couldn't do anything, because your body had a considered opinion about this and expressed it by not moving.

You sit there until your coffee is finished. Then you leave.
</freezeText>


